experiment: axisphase
  instrument        axisphase   trial seed 00000000013580dc   1214 trials per arm
                    another probe is another trial sequence: its numbers
                    are not comparable with these (~0.05 at 300 trials)
  axisphase -- two instants of one gesture, two lessons. Every refused run
  asked for two values of ONE formant, read through ONE centroid. Does it
  work when the two lessons are ORTHOGONAL instead?
  why ask      `capacity` measured two orthogonal lessons coexisting where
               same-axis ones collide (0.84 kept vs 0.22), and F1 and F2
               come from two SEPARATE population-coded groups.
  the design   `same` asks both phases about F1 (the refused baseline,
               reproduced in-run). `split` asks phase 0 about F2 only and
               phase 1 about F1 only -- the two halves of /i/, a target
               already proved reachable.
  the quantity IDENTITY minus SWAPPED, on BOTH formants, paired on seed.
               A taught separation is NEGATIVE, the same convention
               `phaseteach` uses, so the baseline arm is comparable.
  the bar      pre-registered: `split` must clear 3 SE on the axis each
               phase was taught, where `same` sits under 1 SE. Splitting
               what no run has split -- a lesson cannot address an INSTANT,
               versus two lessons cannot share ONE CENTROID.
  refused if   `split` is null too. Then the instant is the binding
               constraint, independently of the axis collision.


  arm           n  F1 ph1-ph0         F2 ph1-ph0          share    off lesson drop         
